Use a 256-entry device lookup table for E4M3FN decoding in the SM80 split-KV
speculative verifier.

SM80 cannot lower Triton's native fp8e4nv load.  The original compatibility
path therefore rebuilt every FP8 value with integer masks and tl.exp2 inside
the hottest long-context loop.  Every finite E4M3FN value is exactly
representable in BF16, so a 512-byte BF16 table has identical semantics, stays
hot in cache, and also avoids an FP32-to-BF16 conversion per lane.  The table is
allocated once with the verifier workspace, so its address is stable across
CUDA Graph capture and replay.

The exhaustive 256-code check maps the two E4M3FN NaN encodings to zero, just
like the previous fail-closed decoder.  On a CMP 170HX, q=8 and 32 segments,
the isolated partial+combine path improved by roughly 1.4x at 4K and 1.5x at
126K/250K, with bit-identical BF16 outputs.

Apply after spec-decode-fp8-kv-sm80.patch.

